    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a clean, additive soft-delete:\n\n- `Owner.deleted` is placed alongside the other state fields (`possibleDuplicateOf`), defaults to `false`, and gets a conventional `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()` pair with a focused Javadoc \u2014 idiomatic JPA entity style matching the surrounding code.\n- The DELETE handler's switch from `deleteOwner(owner)` to `owner.setDeleted(true); saveOwner(owner)` is the natural mutate-then-persist Spring idiom, and reuses the existing service method rather than introducing a parallel one.\n- The two create-time checks (`requireUniqueIdentity`, `requireNoHouseholdDuplicate`) each gain the `!existing.isDeleted()` filter inline at the exact stream that performs the check \u2014 logic stays in the unit that owns it, and neither method becomes a catch-all.\n- Schema changes are mirrored consistently across all four DB dialects, and the OpenAPI `deleted` property (readOnly, documented) is consistent with the sibling fields.\n\nThe `!existing.isDeleted()` predicate does appear in two places, but at only two occurrences extracting a shared `activeOwners()` helper or predicate constant would be marginal \u2014 inlining it at each check site keeps each method self-contained and readable, which fits how the other stream-based checks in this controller are written. Nothing is duplicated in a way a maintainer would find awkward.",
